s is a slow  burn detective thriller in the same vein  as david fincher's seven or more  directly it's a murder mystery taking  heavy cues from the long halloween and  dark victory comics with some subtle  nods to even batman hush it shows batman  the world's greatest detective actually  doing detective things as the primary  driving force of the film coupled with  batman's introspective narrative that  underpins so many moments across this  film and you have such a phenomenally  faithful incarnation of this character  and so i think regardless of whether you  like this take the best or not it is  unquestionably right off of the pages  and personally i can't praise it enough  for that thankfully it is also a great  film in its own right and not just a  series of references in a row to appease  fans the cat and mouse game between the  riddler and batman is incredibly  compelling and thematically competent  and the way matt reeves and the rest of  the writing team have constructed a  young misguided bruce wayne is an  absolute joy to watch topped off by such  an oppressive score by michael giaccino  and you have yourself one hell of a  batman movie it's my feeling that this  is a movie that will appeal to fans old  and new while also being approachable  for ones who might not necessarily love  superhero films so if you or your family  haven't seen it it's a thrilling ride  the likes of which i don't believe you  will soon forget now spoiler one if you  do not wish to be spoiled then i think  this is time for you to leave and come  back once you've seen the film i will be  touching on almost every major plot  point in this film seriously like  everything and there's a lot to cover so  let's get right into it  the central conflict the plot of this  film is made up of several major factors  at the forefront of the story you have  the riddler who's taking out gotham's  corrupt elite via some truly twisted  methods throwing out riddles that batman  must solve in time intertwined in this  is the story of carmine falcone the  penguin and their involvement in gotham  city's corrupt police force if the  premise of a killer on the loose taking  down mob assets sounds familiar to you  then you'd be correct for all intents  and purposes this is the exact plot of  the long halloween comic and considering  they were literally handing out issues  of number one at the cinema  they're not shy about sharing that  inspiration this time however there is  no holiday killer but instead we have  paul danos riddler who despite not  having much screen time at all gives one  hell of a powerful performance the  incarnation of the character here is  startlingly different from much of his  comic book appearances in terms of tone  in catwoman when in rome he's kind of a  dorky loser and even in comics more  central to him like those found in new  52 or rebirth he's still a bit too campy  and extravagant for a live action movie  such as this and as a result what reeves  has done with this character is quite  the departure and yet also not the  riddler in this movie is presented much  like jigsaw from the saw series he puts  them in awful situations and leaves  riddles that may allow some of them to  be saved yet at the same time his  reasoning is 100 in line with what's  found in the comics he's beaten down by  society he feels unnoticed and bitter  and his sense of self-worth about his  own intelligence and desire to be  noticed grows to psychotic degrees which  was exactly what i found when reading  the four of a kind comic for my batman  forever review however it has been given  a more grounded modern twist riddler's  revealed to have a small online  following only 500 people of which he  manages to radicalize only a few for his  deeds and yet that's just enough to do  significant damage it's not hard to see  the analog here to real-life cases like  elliot roger a deeply disturbed young  killer who found himself glorified by  similarly messed up people who  felt slighted by society batman and by  extension bruce serves as a parallel to  this take this is only year two of his  career and he hasn't quite worked out  what the batman needs to be or even how  to really function as bruce wayne  there's no suave ladies man on show here  this is a very young tortured individual  who repeatedly calls himself vengeance  he feels let down by what the city has  become what's happened to his people and  wants revenge on those who made it this  way  exactly  like our villain the riddler  thematically this is so important and  one of the coolest parts about this  movie one of the major turning points is  a section towards the end where the  riddlers in custody and batman rushes  over believing his identity has been  sussed out due to the countless bruce  wayne indications all throughout his  apartment only it turns out that wasn't  the case in a brilliant bit of  misdirection it's revealed that the  riddler wanted to team up with batman to  take down bruce wayne  someone he saw as the embodiment of  corruption that deeply affected his  childhood as an orphan another clear  parallel by the way this interrogation  scene is spectacular it's revealed that  riddler saw batman as an ally his warp  sense of reality led him to believe that  they were working together all of this  time to clean up gotham's corruption the  riddles weren't meant to be there for  batman to stop the next death they were  there for him to solve thus moving  pieces into place to allow for his next  plan to play out successfully and so now  that you have batman someone riddler saw  as a kindred spirit telling him he's a  maniac psychotic and wrong on every  account he's left with this worldview  completely shattered and dano's acting  here just blew me away he is completely  unrestrained all of the composure  intelligence that marked his performance  up until this point seeps away and  what's left is a pathetic lunatic quite  literally screeching it's the moment  where ridler's quote-unquote mask drops  off and we finally get to see the  sniveling loser we know and love from  the comics it's such a fascinating  portrayal of this character most  critically though it's a powerful moment  of resilience for both characters  particularly batman who begins to come  to terms with how wrong his notions of  vengeance truly are in an earlier  section of this film bruce meets a man  who is beyond done with gotham's  societal failings and this seemingly  one-off character poetically wraps back  around to become one of riddler's  radicalized thugs who upon being  recognized by batman states i'm  vengeance finally making a click that  batman needs to be a symbol of hope and  not one that inspires misguided copycats  seeking revenge it plays off a common  idea that batman may very well be a  large part of creating many of the  villains he so desperately tries to take  off the streets it's a fitting part of  this early story for the batman  character and i absolutely love the way  it was handled it's what this film story  hinges on and they not only pulled this  narrative trick off but they set the  standard in my opinion this is  everything this film needed to be and  more  okay now time to talk about the inner  workings unsurprisingly across this  three-hour run time there's a lot more  at play than just the core concepts i've  outlined so far one of the other central  stories that underpins riddler's plans  is about the mob and their involvement  with not only gotham's police but its  political structure too at the center of  this story is carmine falcone selena  kyle the penguin and bruce wayne not  batman but wayne himself it's here that  the film's numerous comic influences  come out in full force and it even  included a comic reference that aj never  expected to be relevant and so never  told me to read beforehand and that  comic is 2012's batman earth 1 and it  takes elements from it that really  caught me off guard as someone who only  familiarized himself with the more  conventional batman stories in this film  alfred's not the old butler i'm used to  he's younger and much tougher with a  military background portrayed  wonderfully by andy serkis but most  critically what it takes from earth 1 is  the plot point that martha wayne why did  you say that  spend time at arkham and her mother  suffered a mental breakdown killing her  husband and then herself it merges this  with a plot point from the long  halloween where thomas wayne saves  falcone's life and owes him a favor as a  result it creates this plot point where  bruce believes his father to be corrupt  working with falcone to have a reporter  killed who was going to spill the beans  on martha's dark past an act that would  ultimately cause martha and thomas's  device and with a film like this which  is built upon shattering preconceived  notions of the world  it's a perfect source to draw from with  the riddler revealing this to the city  it completely devastates bruce who was  entirely under the impression his  parents were saints he loses faith in  himself and his reasons for what he's  doing and he also loses faith in alfred  believing he's been lied to his whole  life it results in one of the most  touching scenes in the film where  circles and pattinson  really get to flex their acting chops as  they reconcile and alfred explains the  reality of this situation bruce's father  never meant to have anyone killed in a  moment of weakness he turned to falcone  to try and scare the reporter only for  falcone to go all the way he explains  that thomas wanted to go hand himself in  and tell the police everything only to  end up dead possibly at the hands of  falcone possibly by pure coincidence by  a desperate criminal it's left  unanswered something also present in the  long halloween it's a different take  from the tale of joe chill the man  responsible for their deaths in the  nolan movies and a huge number of batman  comics dating back all the way to the  original series in the 40s to have this  big unknown cast a shadow over the  motivator behind the batman is hugely  relevant here and ties into the  conclusion of this movie that batman  should not be a symbol of vengeance are  you seeing how everything is tying  together yet and i think that's what  makes this movie so different from all  the other adaptations over the years so  many of the live-action batman movies  are focused on the villains but this  movie is so so much more focused and  grounded around the character of batman  that it's hard to find ways to touch on  anyone else succinctly selena kyle plays  a prominent role here for example taking  on the look of her character from batman  year one but with a backstory straight  out of dark victory and the new 52 with  carmine falcone being her actual father  he even gets his iconic face scratches  too which i thought was pretty dope i  think what she does best for the  narrative though is the way she  indirectly confronts bruce wayne about  his background she pushes batman on his  reactions to certain situations saying  it's clear he grew up rich and it's an  aspect of the character that seldom  touched on how does someone who grew up  so deeply privileged even begin to  understand the people driven to crime  out of reasons like desperation she  facilitates the way this film makes  batman feel human and this is such an  early take on the character he routinely  messes up he can't just tank bullets and  he often relies on the help of others to  set him straight outside of selena the  most prominent person to provide said  help is james gordon in many ways he's  the main character right alongside  batman sharing possibly equal screen  time jeffrey wright is such a great  person for this role too by the way he's  got that real hard edge about him much  like the year one comic showing off how  someone so good can handle himself in a  department so crooked he helps ground  batman while from a filmmaking  standpoint simultaneously serves to  showcase batman's genius they share  crime scenes together tackling clues and  riddles and where gordon and the other  police officers struggle batman's there  to piece things together in an instant  their body cop antics are also a huge  source of comedy in this film despite  how dark this film can be and often is  it also manages to feel appropriately  funny without jarring against the  overall tone the thumb drive gag the  pull your punch gag the classic  disappearing act they have such awesome  chemistry together a chemistry i don't  think has ever been committed to the  screen so convincingly and with that  said i think the only real place that  this film falters is in its choice of  finale as i mentioned earlier this film  is a slow burn detective thriller for  about two and a half hours of its run  time its action is fairly minimal with  an emphasis on the cerebral aspects of  each character it barely feels like a  superhero film and yet when the  riddler's plan is blown open it  immediately transitions into a city-wide  level threat it's a huge scale event on  par with the dark knight rises final act  that feels a little at odds with how  low-key the film has been prior to this  point i think by and large though this  is a small complaint while it is a large  event it's not a snyder level  punch-a-thon special effects  extravaganza the way the film focuses  itself is concentrated enough that it  comes across more like a slice of a  disaster movie more so than anything  else and what it does thematically is  what's most important after all the fact  is batman may have won in the sense that  he's learned what he needs to to be a  symbol for gotham but loses the conflict  in this film he didn't catch the riddler  he let himself be caught he solved the  riddles too late gotham city is left  completely destroyed parts are cut off  people are left desperate and isolated  it's like the setup for the comic no  man's land and that's hugely exciting  for the future of this franchise if  reeves continues at the helm and that's  where my excitement comes from here for  the future the future of this franchise  has me incredibly excited off the back  of this wonderful film there are a huge  number of hints towards future content  throughout and i can't wait to see how  they play out i've not even mentioned  colin farrell's penguin despite the fact  that it's one of the most stand out  performances in this film and some of  the best transformative acting in recent  memory the fact he's getting his own hbo  spin-off series that focuses harder on  the corrupt aspects of gotham's police  system is so exciting then of course  there's also the part where batman  injects himself with this green fluid  was it just adrenaline was it venom  hinting at bane or the addiction story  from the comics of the same name heck  there's even what appears to be a joker  cameo at one point this film is just  packed full of detail i cannot emphasize  enough just how much i adore it i do  genuinely think this may very well be my  favorite batman film to date now that  may be recency bias talking but the way  it brings the source material to the big  screen is just so uncompromising and  perfectly accurate and with such a  compelling story and awesome  performances to boot i can just barely  fault it if i had to put a score in it i  would probably go with a 9 out of 10 a  strong 9 out of 10. i think also because  of this it means retroactively it  affects some of the scores that came  before most critically sending halle  berry's catwoman into the negatives  justice in conclusion it is my firm  belief that all-time comic fans will  love this incredible three-hour  spectacular that doesn't feel at all  like three hours as always subscribe for  more reviews like this and i'll see you  all next time thanks for watching  [Music]  you

